WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.399
This work, from AWS AI and UC Santa Barbara,

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:09.762
is titled "Automatic Clipping: Differentially
Private Deep Learning Made Easier and Stronger."

00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:15.720
Differentially private training of deep networks
relies on per-sample gradient clipping,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:22.761
but the clipping threshold R is a fragile
hyperparameter that must be tuned carefully for good accuracy.

00:00:22.841 --> 00:00:25.401
The authors propose automatic clipping,

00:00:25.481 --> 00:00:30.005
a drop-in replacement that removes R entirely from
any DP optimizer,

00:00:30.085 --> 00:00:37.790
so private training becomes as tuning-friendly as
ordinary training while matching or beating the state of the art.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:42.527
In differentially private deep learning,

00:00:42.607 --> 00:00:46.964
every per-sample gradient is clipped to a fixed
norm R before noise is added,

00:00:47.044 --> 00:00:51.401
and that single threshold R turns out to be
decisive for accuracy.

00:00:51.481 --> 00:00:55.839
Picking it wrong is costly: on ImageNet,

00:00:55.919 --> 00:01:00.276
ResNet18 accuracy can collapse from forty-five
percent to thirty-one percent when R is merely doubled.

00:01:00.356 --> 00:01:04.713
State-of-the-art private models tend to need very
small clipping thresholds that can only be found through careful,

00:01:04.793 --> 00:01:09.150
expensive tuning.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:14.538
Searching jointly over the clipping threshold and
the learning rate is one of the main reasons DP training is painful.

00:01:14.618 --> 00:01:19.627
For large models this grid search can take days to
months of compute,

00:01:19.707 --> 00:01:24.715
and because it inspects private data it also spends
additional privacy budget.

00:01:24.795 --> 00:01:29.803
Crucially,

00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:34.892
the best thresholds are usually so small that
nearly every per-sample gradient is clipped at every step,

00:01:34.972 --> 00:01:39.980
which hints that the precise value of R may not
matter at all if we reformulate the clipping.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.593
The authors make four contributions.

00:01:44.673 --> 00:01:48.906
First, they propose automatic clipping,

00:01:48.986 --> 00:01:53.219
which mathematically expunges the clipping
threshold from general DP optimizers including DP-SGD,

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:57.532
DP-Adam, and DP-LAMB.

00:01:57.612 --> 00:02:01.845
Second,

00:02:01.925 --> 00:02:06.158
they prove that automatic DP-SGD converges in the
non-convex setting at the same asymptotic rate as standard SGD.

00:02:06.238 --> 00:02:10.471
Third, they show any positive constant threshold is
equivalent to setting it to one,

00:02:10.551 --> 00:02:14.784
so a single default suffices.

00:02:14.864 --> 00:02:19.097
And fourth, they demonstrate superior results
across vision and language benchmarks,

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:23.410
achievable by changing a single line of code in
popular libraries.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.762
The idea starts from a simple observation: when the
threshold is small,

00:02:28.842 --> 00:02:33.956
Abadi's clipping factor, the minimum of R over the
gradient norm and one,

00:02:34.036 --> 00:02:37.300
is almost always just R over the gradient norm.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:43.063
So the authors drop the minimum entirely and
normalize every per-sample gradient,

00:02:43.143 --> 00:02:45.838
a variant they call AUTO-V for vanilla.

00:02:45.918 --> 00:02:50.320
This maximizes alignment between the private and
true gradient,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.368
but it makes all gradients the same size, creating
a lazy region where updates stall.

00:02:56.448 --> 00:03:01.562
To fix this they add a small stability constant
gamma in the denominator,

00:03:01.642 --> 00:03:05.617
giving AUTO-S: R divided by the gradient norm plus gamma.

00:03:05.697 --> 00:03:11.238
This preserves relative magnitudes, letting small
gradients shrink toward zero.

00:03:11.318 --> 00:03:16.503
Finally, because any constant threshold simply
rescales the learning rate,

00:03:16.583 --> 00:03:21.413
they fix R to one, and set gamma to a default of
zero point zero one,

00:03:21.493 --> 00:03:24.330
leaving a fully threshold-free optimizer.

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.119
The method is tested broadly.

00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:34.414
On language, the authors finetune RoBERTa base and
large on the GLUE tasks MNLI, QQP,

00:03:34.494 --> 00:03:35.787
QNLI, and SST-2,

00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:43.597
and finetune GPT2 in three sizes for table-to-text
generation on the E2E and DART datasets.

00:03:43.677 --> 00:03:48.832
On vision, they evaluate CIFAR-10 with a pretrained
SimCLRv2,

00:03:48.912 --> 00:03:53.467
ImageNette with a ResNet9, and ImageNet with ResNet18.

00:03:53.547 --> 00:04:01.019
They use standard privacy budgets of epsilon three
and epsilon eight and reuse the exact

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:05.053
hyperparameters of prior state-of-the-art work,

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:07.370
changing only the clipping.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:14.058
Across the board, automatic clipping matches or
outperforms the state of the art,

00:04:14.138 --> 00:04:17.922
and it does so without ever tuning the threshold.

00:04:18.002 --> 00:04:20.446
For GPT2 text generation on E2E,

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:26.361
AUTO-S reaches a BLEU score of sixty-four point one
eight at epsilon three,

00:04:26.441 --> 00:04:31.013
edging past the prior best of sixty-three point
eight five.

00:04:31.093 --> 00:04:36.770
For RoBERTa-base on SST-2, it reaches ninety-two
point three two percent,

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.162
above the prior ninety-one point eight six.

00:04:40.242 --> 00:04:47.496
On CIFAR-10 with a pretrained SimCLRv2 it hits
ninety-two point seven percent at epsilon two.

00:04:47.576 --> 00:04:51.518
And because only the learning rate needs searching,

00:04:51.598 --> 00:04:54.830
the tuning cost drops by about five times.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:59.109
Two ablations anchor the design.

00:04:59.189 --> 00:05:03.088
First,

00:05:03.168 --> 00:05:07.067
comparing AUTO-V and AUTO-S shows that once the
small stability constant restores gradient magnitude,

00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:11.046
AUTO-S consistently wins, confirming that the lazy
region really does hurt AUTO-V.

00:05:11.126 --> 00:05:15.025
Second,

00:05:15.105 --> 00:05:19.004
sweeping the stability constant gamma shows the
method is essentially insensitive to it:

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:22.983
any positive gamma gives the same asymptotic
convergence rate,

00:05:23.063 --> 00:05:26.962
which is why a single default of zero point zero
one works everywhere.

00:05:27.042 --> 00:05:30.941
Heatmaps over threshold and learning rate further
show the AUTO-S result landing right

00:05:31.021 --> 00:05:34.920
at the best hand-tuned threshold.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:40.236
The headline numbers: GPT2 on E2E reaches BLEU
sixty-four point one eight at epsilon three,

00:05:40.316 --> 00:05:45.252
versus sixty-three point eight five for the prior best.

00:05:45.332 --> 00:05:50.268
RoBERTa-base on SST-2 reaches ninety-two point
three two percent,

00:05:50.348 --> 00:05:55.284
and RoBERTa-large on the same task reaches
ninety-four point six one percent at epsilon eight.

00:05:55.364 --> 00:06:00.300
CIFAR-10 with SimCLRv2 hits ninety-two point seven
percent at epsilon two.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:05.316
The sensitivity of the old approach is stark: on ImageNet,

00:06:05.396 --> 00:06:10.332
doubling the threshold drops ResNet18 accuracy from
forty-five to thirty-one percent.

00:06:10.412 --> 00:06:15.348
Theoretically,

00:06:15.428 --> 00:06:20.364
the minimum expected gradient norm shrinks at the
rate T to the minus one quarter,

00:06:20.444 --> 00:06:25.380
matching standard SGD.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.204
The lasting message is that the clipping threshold,

00:06:29.284 --> 00:06:33.833
long treated as a critical knob in differentially
private training,

00:06:33.913 --> 00:06:35.353
can simply be removed.

00:06:35.433 --> 00:06:40.674
By normalizing each per-sample gradient and adding
a tiny stability constant,

00:06:40.754 --> 00:06:44.750
you get an optimizer that is just as private, just
as fast,

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.343
and just as accurate as the best hand-tuned methods,

00:06:48.423 --> 00:06:52.212
backed by a convergence guarantee matching standard SGD.

00:06:52.292 --> 00:06:55.391
It is a one-line change in existing libraries,

00:06:55.471 --> 00:07:00.020
which finally makes DP training about as easy as
ordinary training.
